  "narrative_text": "Four independent pathway-enrichment paradigms converge on a single dominant biological signal: perturbation of tryptophan metabolism. RaMP-DB ORA returned KEGG:map00380 'Tryptophan metabolism' as its top hit with an FDR of 8.59e-19 and fold-enrichment of 875.7, covering seven of the twelve input metabolites (tryptamine, indoleacetaldehyde, 3-hydroxyanthranilic acid, serotonin, L-tryptophan, quinolinic acid, melatonin). MetaboAnalystR KSEA confirmed the same KEGG:hsa00380 pathway (p = 5.41e-09, 6 metabolites), and Mummichog's empirical-compound network assigned it the lowest p-value (0.00193) with an almost complete 16/17 compound overlap. WikiPathways variants WP:WP465 and WP:WP4210 (NAD+ biosynthesis from tryptophan) appeared in RaMP as secondary hits with FDR < 1e-10. The driver metabolites span three functional tiers of the pathway: the central substrate L-tryptophan (KEGG:C00078), key neuroactive intermediates serotonin (C00780) and melatonin (C01598), and the kynurenine-branch effectors 3-hydroxyanthranilic acid (C00632) and quinolinic acid (C03722). A secondary marginal signal in the Mummichog output links dihydrolipoate (C02147) to ascorbate/vitamin C and aldarate metabolism (p=0.054), suggesting mitochondrial redox involvement. The mycotoxin 2,3-epoxyaflatoxin B1 (C19586) and acetylcholine (C01996) did not reach pathway significance in any paradigm, indicating they are either peripheral contributors or biological noise. The tryptophan metabolism enrichment is consistent across ORA (hypergeometric), network-empirical (m/z), and multi-database consensus approaches, establishing it as the primary metabolic response to the treatment.",
